{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1554259-your-favorite-v-tm-characters-by-edition","id":1554259,"title":"Your favorite V:TM characters by Edition","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-10-09T06:35:54-07:00","id":1554259,"title":"Your favorite V:TM characters by Edition","content":"This is pretty much what the title says. You list out your favorite NPCs from each of the editions of Vampire: The Masquerade. Some of them have updates that allow them to fit into multiple categories. For the sake of the discussion, V20 is also &quot;Fourth Edition.&quot;<br /> <br /> You can do it by how characters affected you, their usefulness in Chronicles, or simply their vibe. You can also name as many as you want.<br /> <br /> <u>1st Edition</u><br /> <br /> <b>The Gary Crew</b>: I know, I know, who could have possibly known I would choose this collection of characters and I'm cheating by choosing them all but I think it's a fascinating coterie of characters that covers almost the entirety of the spectrum of vampires by itself. You have the has-been Prince acting like he's a 17th century nobleman, his enslaved Charlotte Bronte daughter, Juggler as the Anarch signifying nothing but full of hot air, his own enslaved daughter, a Golconda seeker stuck in the middle of nowhere, and Lucian representing a Methuselah who managed to live for millennia by not gaining supreme power. Plus, Michael, who is an innocent but you KNOW is a walking Masquerade breach. Even Sullivan Dane qualifies here.<br /> <br /> <u>2nd Edition</u><br /> <br /> <b>Lucita</b>: The &quot;you're basic&quot; V:TM answer, like asking if what your favorite music is and saying Taylor Swift or Metallica. I have no regrets. Lucita is just the perfect encapsulation of V:TM at its trenchcoat and katanas best (I love Christof for the same reason). We all basically think of Selene as somewhat similar and she has all that appeal but is also still pretty evil. If she had debuted as a comic book character in the Nineties, she'd probably still be running.<br /> <br /> <u>Revised</u><br /> <br /> <b>Christof</b>: Vampire: The Masquerade Redemption is an overlooked classic and while not as iconic as Bloodlines, I think this game was perfect in its own ways. He has the Connor Macleod vibes of a sword wielding immortal wandering around town fighting evil. Do I like the Dark Ages segment over the Modern Day and kind of wish they were two games? Yes, but I still love the ridiculous caste of misfits he attracted.<br /> <br /> <b>The Malkavian Fledgling (Bloodlines)</b>: This is also something of a cheating answer or an answer with caveats because there's no reason the Malkavian should work as well as they do. They're the ultimate Fishmalk. But what works for a solo play is far better than an entire Clan or even as the disruptive character in a coterie. Whether a sexy cheerleader or Doctor Seuss pimp, they are just so much fun.<br /> <br /> <u>V20</u><br /> <br /> <b>Dastur Anosh: </b>An odd pick, I know, and requires you to deeply immerse yourself in the lore to a point you're drowning. I also think he exists primarily to explain the oddity of why the Path of Caine resembles the Path of Blood so much despite the two being irreconcilable. However, I love this depiction of a tortured theologian and cultist who basically is everything the Sabbat claims (enlightened posthuman warrior poets) to be but rarely actually ever approaches.<br /> <br /> <b>The Red Question</b>: 5th Edition went an entirely different way than V20 but I think V20 had the better idea of having the internet affacted by vampire hackers due to the issue of MAGIC(!). The Nosferatu and Red Question being able to scrub so much. Also, the idea of Anarchs finally cottoning to the idea that the current system of rebellion was controlled opposition.<br /> <br /> <u>5th Edition</u><br /> <br /> <b>LA By Night Crew</b>: While not hitting the same level as Critical Role, I enjoyed this group tremendously. I still would have been a fan of V5 due to the work of Onyx Path on Chicago by Night, London by Night, and Cults of the Blood Gods but I loved all of their characters. Maybe they didn't sling enough dice but they seemed to have a huge amount of fun and that's really the important part to me.<br /> <br /> <b>Kevin Jackson</b>: I really liked the way the character was updated for 5th Edition and you can tell he's either going to ascend to become a fantastic elder or crash and burn like Icarus (with the Sun burning up his vampire corpse too). Kevin shows exactly how far you can get in the Post-Beckoning World and the Camarilla but he's also a guy who really is still just a pawn of Helena and the Elders around him. He achieved much of what he did through hard work and skill but...well, also a perfect storm of events that will probably not last. The undead world is not fair and does not reward idealism.<br /> <br /> <b>Dolph</b>: The Gentleman Gamer's character in an adaptation of &quot;The Sacrifice&quot; by Red Moon Roleplaying. He's a terrifying Ministry Model that is an abusive controlling boyfriend and diablerist that pretty much would be a perfect Toreador but has the Ministry's casual corruption going on too. Seriously, fantastic character.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www